  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nDry northwest flow conditions expected today in the wake of a \ncold front that crossed the area Wednesday evening. High \ntemperatures today will average about 10 degrees cooler than the\nprevious day. Surface high pressure will start out over the \nUpper Midwest this morning before gradually building into Ohio \nthis evening. The pressure gradient will support breezy \nnorthwest winds, gusting to 15-20 mph this afternoon. In \naddition, relative humidity will tend to drop to 25-30 percent \nin Northwest Ohio which could support fire spread if any were to\ndevelop. Fuels are considered to be a little too wet to need a \nfire weather headline. The flow off Lake Erie will keep humidity\nvalues slightly higher across the eastern half of the forecast \narea. \n\nHigh pressure and clear skies tonight will allow for good \nradiational cooling. Inland temperatures will drop to the lower 40s \nand can not rule out a couple of the typically cooler spots reaching \nthe upper 30s. This should be just slightly too warm for frost \nformation. Temperatures will trend warmer Friday into Saturday as \nthe upper level trough moves off the New England Coast and the upper \nlevel ridge expands north into the Ohio Valley. Several areas across \nNorthwest Ohio and over to Cleveland could reach 80 degrees on \nSaturday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nHurricane Isaias has developed in the Caribbean \nand is forecast to track north to the Alabama Coast by late Friday. \nThe system will weaken after making landfall but is expected to \ntrack slightly northwest towards the Mid-Mississippi Valley on \nSaturday as strong high pressure remains over New England. Moisture \nassociated with this system will spread north ahead of the low, with \nrain beginning from south to north across the local area late \nSaturday night into Sunday morning. At this time the heaviest swath \nof rain is forecast to be along and south of Route 30. Forecast rain \namounts range from a half inch towards Toledo to just over an inch \ncloser to Central Ohio. There will also be a low end threat of \nthunderstorms across mainly Central and Northeast Ohio. We will need \nto keep an eye on downsloping winds towards Erie, PA ahead of this \nsystem which could exceed 35 mph, depending on the track of the \nsystem. We will continue to monitor rainfall potential but at this \ntime flooding is not expected. Given the fairly widespread nature of \nthe rain, trended high temperatures down a couple degrees for \nSunday. We will see a mid-level dry slot try to wrap north into the \nsystem on Sunday afternoon which could reduce the coverage of \nshowers. Pops remain into Sunday night for all but extreme\nNorthwest Ohio.",
